"""Written-out counts, rather than Arabic digit tokens.

English counts two through ten are mostly negative; compound-number endings
and magnitudes (twenty-five, three hundred) are positive. Ranges, lists and
counts following ordinals have weaker responses. Chinese numeral tokens are
negative too, especially with classifiers, but adjacent written numerals are
positive. A numeral at a document's first position is treated separately.
State keeps only the preceding word and a short number-phrase continuation;
it resets at each document. Case and token-boundary adjustments damp word
fragments. Everything unrecognized is predicted as zero.
